<worksheet xml:id="ws-1">
  <title>Introduction to Differential Equations</title>
<objectives> 

  <ul>
    <li>Basic DEQ models</li>
    <li>Basic DEQ terminology</li>
    <li><q>Rate</q> versus <q>Solution curve</q></li> analysis
  </ul>
</objectives>
<page>
  <p>
  <alert>Directions:</alert> Work in groups of three to four students on this
  activity. Each group shall submit one solution consisting of a copy of this
  handout with neatly handwritten solutions. Extra copies of this handout are
  available at the course web site.
</p>
<p>
  Differential equations are in important tool for modeling phenomena in the biological, physical,
and social sciences. The purpose of this activity is to gain experience constructing elementary differential equations, to become familiar with basic differential equations terminology, and to learn certain graphical solution techniques.  
</p>
<p>Consider the following situations. For each situation, construct a differential equation or an initial value problem that mathematically describes the situation.	
    </p>>
<exercise workspace="2.0in">
  <introduction/>
  
    <statement>Carbon-14 is a radioactive isotope that decays in such a way that the
  instantaneous rate of decay of the amount of Carbon-14 is directly
  proportional to the amount of Carbon-14 present. Let <m>A</m> denote the
  amount of Carbon-14 present after <m>t</m> years, and let <m>k</m> denote your
  constant of proportionality.</statement>
   
 
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="2.0in">
  <introduction><p>
      Similarly, a sample of <em>e.coli</em> bacteria grows in such a way that the
  instantaneous rate of growth of the amount of <em>e.coli</em> is directly
  proportional to the amount of <em>e.coli</em> present.</p>>
  <p>
  Let's take a closer look at your differential equation from (b). Since
  <m>P\geq 0</m>, then <m>\frac{dP}{dt}\geq 0</m>.</p></introduction>
  <task>
   <p>What does this derivative condition imply about the solution graph of
  <m>P</m>?</p>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What does your response to the previous question imply in turn about whether
  <m>\frac{dP}{dt}</m> is increasing or decreasing? What does this in turn imply
  about the concavity of the solution function <m>P</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
 
<exercise workspace="2.0in">
  <introduction><p>
      he population growth model <m>\displaystyle \frac{dP}{dt}=kP</m> from the
  previous two examples is sometimes refered to as the exponential or <alert>Malthusian </alert> model of population growth, named after the British political
  economist Thomas Robert Malthus (1766-1834). A different, slightly more
  complicated model that factors in limited resources or an
  <q>environmental carrying capacity</q> is the <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\underbar"--><c>\underbar</c></TODO>
  logistic model developed by Pierre-Francois Verhulst (1804-1840). It involves
  two parameters <m>k</m> and <m>N</m>, where <m>k</m> denotes the usual
  proportionality constant and where <m>N</m> represents the maximum possible
  population amount. In this model, the instantaneous rate of change of the
  population is directly proportional to the
  <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\underbar"--><c>\underbar</c></TODO>
  product of two quantities: the population itself at that instant and the
  fraction of the remaining available population amount. Again, let <m>P</m>
  denote the amount of <em>e.coli</em> present after <m>t</m> hours. Assume that
  <m>P(0)=\frac{N}{4}</m>. Next to your initial value problem sketch on
  appropriately labeled axes what you think a plausible graph of the solution
  curve might look like.</p></introduction>
  </exercise>
</page>

<page>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Newton's Law of Cooling is a famous example of a differential equation model
  governing how an object's temperature changes when it is introduced into a
  hotter or cooler environment. In this model the rate of change of the
  temperature of the object is directly proportional to the difference between
  the object's temperature and the temperature of the surrounding environment.
  Suppose I bring a hot, 90 degree Celsius cup of coffee into my 20 degree
  Celsius MTH 304 classroom. Let <m>C</m> denote the temperature of my cup of
  coffee after <m>t</m> minutes, and assume that the constant of proportionality
  is given by <m>k=-.15</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
  </exercise>
</page>
<page>
  <definition>
  <statement>A
  differential equation is an equation involving the derivative of some unknown
  function. The function itself is a rule involves an independent (input) and
  dependent (variable) variable that obeys the function definition. Sometimes
  for the sake of notational simplicity we <q>supress</q> the independent
  variable when we write the differential equation. For example, we can write
  the pregnant wife differential equation as
  <md>
    \frac{dA}{dt}=2.5-kA \mbox{ or as }\frac{dA(t)}{dt}=2.5-kA(t).
  </md>
  Both equations mean the same thing; in each equation <m>t</m> represents the
  independent variable, <m>A</m> the dependent variable, and <m>k</m> a
  parameter. The form on the left, which I may sometimes call the
  <q>supressed form,</q> is easier to write, whereas the form on the right is
  good to keep in mind when we wish to think about the behavior of the dependent
  variable <m>A</m> at a particular <m>t</m> value. The next problem
  demonstrates this idea by showing how we can use the differential equation to
  determine useful information about the solution function without solving the
  differential equation itself.</statement>
</definition>
<exercise>
 <introduction>
      <p> For the cooling cup of coffee initial value problem 4, sketch on
  appropriately labeled axes what you think a solution graph might look like.
  Then use your differential equation to calculate the rate at which the coffee
  is cooling at time <m>t=0</m>. From this result, construct the equation of the
  tangent line to the graph of the solution curve at <m>t=0</m>. Then use this
  result to estimate the temperature of the coffee at time <m>t=2</m> minutes.
   </p>
    </introduction>
</exercise>
</page>
<page> 
  
  <definition>
  <statement>
The <em>order</em> of a differential equation is the highest-order derivative
  that the equation contains. In the first chapter we will only be concerned
  only with first-order differential equations. Such equations can always be
  written in the form <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=f(t,y)</m>, where <m>f</m>
  is a function of two variables. (Remember MTH 203?!) For example, in the
  differential equation <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=y+t</m>, we see that
  <m>f(t,y)=y+t</m>.
    </statement>
</definition>

 <definition>
  <statement>
For a first order differential equation written in <q>supressed form</q>
        it can sometimes be the case <m>f</m> is a function of the dependent
        variable alone. In this case we say that the differential equation is
        <em>autonomous</em>. For example,
        <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=2y+1</m> and
        <m>\displaystyle \frac{dA}{dt}=2.5-kA</m> are both autonomous since (
        <m>k</m> is just a parameter).
    </statement>
</definition>

 <definition>
  <statement>
If the function <m>f</m> can be written in the form
        <m>f(t,y)=m(t)y+b(t)</m>, we say that the differential equation is
        <em>linear</em>. For example, <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=y+t</m> is
        a first-order, linear (nonautonomous) differential equation.v
    </statement>
</definition>

<exercise workspace="3.0in">
  <introduction>
      <p> For each of <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=t-y</m> and (1)-(4), determine
  whether the differential equation is autonomous and whether the differential
  equation is linear.
   </p>
    </introduction>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
  <p>
  
  Differential equations models that are developed in real life frequently are
  quite complicated and cannot be solved explicitly using algebraic methods.
  Instead one must resort to other means to draw conclusions about the
  solutions. There are a variety of techniques we will use over the course of
  the semester, many of them graphical in nature. Because we will discuss a
  variety of graphical approaches, it is imperative that when constructing
  graphs, you
  <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\underbar"--><c>\underbar</c></TODO>
  always label your axes appropriately.
</p>

<p>
  For autonomous differential equations, which can be written in the form
  <m>\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dt}=f(y)</m>, one can construct a curve known as
  the <q>rate</q> versus <q>solution curve</q> plot. This graph is nothing more
  than a plot of the function <m>f(y)</m>, i.e. a plot of the solution curve
  <m>y</m> on the horizonal axes and the rate or derivative
  <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=f(y)</m> on the vertical axes.
</p>
<p>
  For example, the differential equation
  <md>
    \frac{dy}{dt}=.5y(y-2)(y-3)
  </md>
  has a rate versus solution curve given by the graph shown.
</p>

<image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="Q5">
    <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition>f(x)=0.5*x*(x-2)*(x-3)</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,1.5)">
    <grid-axes xlabel="y" ylabel="\frac{dy}{dx}"/>
    <graph at="graph" function="f"/>
    <point p="(0.78475, 1.05631)" size="2">   </point>
    <point p="(2.54858, -0.31557)" size="2">  </point>
    <label anchor="(2.54858, -0.31557)" alignment="south"  scale="0.6">
      (2.5,-0.32)
    </label>
    <label anchor="(0.78475, 1.05631)" alignment="north"  scale="0.6">
      (0.78, 1.06)
    </label>
  </coordinates>

  <annotations text="The graph of a cubic function starting in the third quadrant, increases 
   through the origin to reach a local maximum at the point 
   (0.78, 1.06), then decreases passing through the point (2,0)
   and reaches a local minimum at the point (2.5,-0.32). 
   It increases afterwards passing through the point (3,0)">
    
  </annotations>
</diagram>
  </prefigure>
</image>
<p>
  Suppose that an initial value is given by <m>y(0)=1</m>. What does this graph
  tell us about the solution <m>y(t)</m> as <m>t</m> increases? Let's think
  about this question carefully.
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        When <m>y=1</m> on the rate graph above, the derivative
        <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=y'(t)</m> is positive, implying the solution function
        <m>y(t)</m> is initially increasing from <m>y=1</m>.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        If <m>y(t)</m> is increasing from 1, yet less than 2, the rate graph
        tells us that <m>y'(t)</m> remains positive and decreasing. This implies
        that the solution function <m>y(t)</m> is increasing and concave down.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        The closer <m>y(t)</m> becomes to <m>y=2</m>, the closer <m>y'(t)</m>
        becomes to zero. In essence, <m>y=2</m> is a horizontal asymptote.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  In summary, starting from the point <m>(0,1)</m>, a solution curve would
  increase asymptotically in a concave down manner to <m>y=2</m>.
  <q>What if <m>y(0)=2</m> instead,</q> you say? In this case the solution would
  be a constant solution, i.e. <m>y(t)=2</m> for <m>t \geq 0</m>. We call
  <m>y=2</m> an <em>equilibrium solution</em> of the differential equation. (See
  p. 5 of our textbook)
</p>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
      <p> List the other equilibrium solutions of the differential equation above.
   </p>
    </introduction>
</exercise>
</page>

